A growing hunger for scents good enough to eat

While we=E2=80=99d all love to start each day with a bowl of vanilla ice cr=
eam, that=E2=80=99s frustratingly not recommended by most physicians.

But, as always, consumerism has found a loophole: Fragrances that smell lik=
e indulgent treats.

Gourmand scents, as they=E2=80=99re known, aren=E2=80=99t new =E2=80=94 a s=
ynthesized vanilla scent was created (https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articl=
es/2025-10-22/is-ozempic-changing-our-sense-of-smell?srnd=3Dphx-businesswee=
k&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9rp0bhsaxuPwZ2mBrkdtpcHlz9jpGFOJoifVpQyCXnAsVB5O4YWq6OCJ=
jsjbXRELyWQEdP2VRnvkCsw-iLPoW2eX1pKA&_hsmi=3D395304030 ) in a German lab in=
 the 1870s =E2=80=94 but they=E2=80=99re trending in a big way.

And one of the possible drivers behind the trend is the growing popularity =
of GLP-1 medications.

Makes scents

While medications such as Ozempic, Wegovy, and Mounjaro suppress users=E2=
=80=99 appetites, they could potentially increase (https://www.theguardian.=
